# Code owners.
#
# GitHub has no per-path WRITE permission, so this is not an access-control list.
# Outside contributors never have write access at all: they fork, and they open a
# pull request. What this file does is make a maintainer's review REQUIRED before a
# pull request touching a listed path can merge, when "Require review from Code
# Owners" is on for `main`. That is the path-scoped control, applied at review time
# rather than at push time.
#
# The paths below are ordered least to most sensitive. Later matches win, so the
# more specific entries at the bottom are the ones that actually bind.
#
# Keep this in sync with the branch ruleset. Setup and reasoning:
# repo-protection.md

# Default owner for everything not matched below.
*                                   @isomorphic-12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supply chain. A dependency change is the highest-leverage way to compromise a
# deployment, and `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` in CI means a lockfile edit
# is load-bearing. Never merge one of these on a skim.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/package.json                       @isomorphic-12
/pnpm-lock.yaml                     @isomorphic-12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI and release. A workflow change can exfiltrate secrets or alter what gets
# deployed. `.github/workflows/cla.yml` uses `pull_request_target`, which runs in
# the BASE repo's context: it deliberately does not check out pull-request code,
# and a change that adds such a checkout is a privilege escalation, not a
# refactor.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.github/                           @isomorphic-12
/.github/workflows/                 @isomorphic-12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment identity and schema. wrangler.template.jsonc plus
# scripts/setup-config.ts decide what every deployment, including the hosted
# one, is configured as. Migrations run against the production database before
# the code that depends on them ships.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/wrangler.template.jsonc            @isomorphic-12
/scripts/setup-config.ts            @isomorphic-12
/migrations/                        @isomorphic-12
/src/db/                            @isomorphic-12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth, tenancy, and authorization. A bug here crosses brains or escalates a
# role. This is the code where "looks right" is not good enough.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/src/auth/                          @isomorphic-12
/src/oauth/                         @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/orgs.ts                    @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/tenants.ts                 @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/identity.ts                @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/provision.ts               @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/org-connect.ts             @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/github.ts                  @isomorphic-12
/src/tools/members.ts               @isomorphic-12
/src/tools/org-onboarding.ts        @isomorphic-12
/src/tools/custom.ts                @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/custom-tools.ts            @isomorphic-12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated artifacts. These are committed AND compiled into the Worker, so a
# hand-edit is a way to ship code that is not in any reviewed source file. CI
# regenerates and diffs them, which catches this, so a pull request that edits a
# generated file directly is the signal to look harder rather than to merge.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/src/lib/app-bundle.generated.ts    @isomorphic-12
/src/lib/brain-template.generated.ts @isomorphic-12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Licensing and governance. Changing these changes the deal, for us and for
# every contributor who already signed the CLA under the old terms.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/LICENSE                            @isomorphic-12
/NOTICE                             @isomorphic-12
/CLA.md                             @isomorphic-12
/GOVERNANCE.md                      @isomorphic-12
/SECURITY.md                        @isomorphic-12
/docs/licensing.md                  @isomorphic-12
/docs/design/open-source-boundary.md @isomorphic-12
